# The Tome of Strahd read through (Ashwin, 2026-10-20: "a mix of Strahd's voice and the narrator, mostly Strahd's with the
# narrator filling in information that he didn't write", and "I should be able to re-trigger that cutscene every time I
# read the tome"). Reading the Tome (story/field_items.gd `read_tome`, the inventory) plays this conversation over the book's
# six pictures every time. The Strahd lines are the words he wrote, in the first person; the Narrator lines are the ones
# from the first script that tell what he left out, kept word for word because they are already voiced
# (audio/voice/narrator). The Strahd lines and the last Narrator line are new and unvoiced. Drafted by Gemini through the
# ai-bridge and edited for canon (docs/story/drafts/tome-strahd-voice/). The Amber Temple vault keeps the line that he
# bargained to stop growing older than her: nothing here says it first. Nothing here names Ireena.

~ read
cutscene tome_conqueror
Narrator: By candlelight you read the steady script of a prince who took this valley four hundred years ago, the elder son of the warrior King Barov and Queen Ravenovia. He calls himself "a prince of the western wars" without pride or regret. He writes like a man tallying the winter stores of a fortress.
Strahd: I have set down my victories, my medals and the honours the court heaped upon my name. A prince is measured by what he holds. Far below, my soldiers cheered among the smoking fields, and I did not turn to look at them.
Strahd: I made the castle that bears my mother's name my seat, and then the quiet came. Peace left nothing to conquer. A kingdom won is merely a house to keep, and I was not bred for keeping house.
Narrator: Between the neat lines of the campaign accounts you hear what the decorated war hero cannot bring himself to write. Armies obeyed him and the court honoured him, and he ached to be loved for himself, and not for what he had won.
cutscene tome_brothers
Strahd: My younger brother Sergei had the gift of being loved without trying. The halls warmed to his step before he had done a single day of service. My brother wore the sun as easily as linen.
Strahd: As the elder son I paid for my rank, my army and every acre of this stone in years and blood. Sergei never kept a winter watch or held a line, yet he was given, for nothing, the love I had wanted all my life.
Strahd: Tatyana came to the castle in white gowns, a young woman with auburn hair, and the light in the hall belonged to her. I had taken a country by force, yet before her quiet grace I found something that could not be commanded.
Narrator: You see how carefully he watched the terrace from the shadow of the corridor, his hand tight on the stone as Sergei gave her a white rose. For the first time in his life the conqueror wanted a gentle thing that could only be given freely.
Strahd: I told myself it was the proper concern of an elder brother and the master of the house. Yet I asked the page why a boy who had never bled for a single field should win so easily what a proven commander is denied.
cutscene tome_refused
Strahd: I put on my dress uniform, heavy with ribbons and campaign medals, and went to Tatyana. I offered her my hand and my house, and laid ten years of hard-won glory before her, judging that a heart, like any fortress, must yield to honours.
Strahd: She heard my offer to the end, and then she declined it with a small, apologetic smile that cut deeper than any cruelty. She looked upon me with pity. I wrote it down in one flat line and closed the book.
Narrator: While she spoke to him her eyes were already across the hall, where Sergei waited among the dancers with his easy laugh. Everything Strahd had won in ten years of war counted for nothing against a young man who had never known a battlefield.
Strahd: That night I resolved the matter. I had endured the winter marches and the iron of my enemies, but I would not endure her pity. What is mine by merit shall not be lost to softness.
cutscene tome_wedding
Strahd: I made the long freezing climb into the high mountains, where a courteous old warden led me before a hollow sarcophagus of amber. In that dark a voice understood me at last. It asked only what I wanted, and I answered without ornament.
Narrator: You read the terms he accepted. He would stay young for as long as he wanted anything, and keep whatever he had taken. The thing in the amber would live in him, and he in it, and he and his land would be one. The price was a life he loved. The page does not say whose.
Strahd: All that week I showed Tatyana every kindness while she chose her white flowers for the feast. The price of my bargain fell due on the very day I was to claim my happiness: a necessary debt, best settled swiftly.
Narrator: Sergei fell at his own wedding feast, in his white coat, and never knew who had struck him. Strahd writes nothing of his own shaking hands above the dropped blade. He will not let himself feel what he has just broken.
cutscene tome_wall
Strahd: Tatyana ran along the high castle walls as the white mist rose from the stones. When I reached for her she looked once into my face and stepped into the fog. She mistook my reach for ruin, and the precipice took what was mine.
Narrator: He tells himself she fled only because the noise and the panic had confused her. Below the wall his own guards, frightened, loose their arrows at him, and the iron goes in. He does not die. He wakes changed, in the dark, as the voice in the amber keeps its word.
Strahd: The grey mist closed round the mountains and sealed the valley shut. The valley and I are one thing now. I keep the accounts of these stones, and I still call my hunger a kingdom.
cutscene tome_again
Narrator: In the centuries that follow his handwriting never alters as he sets down the one torment left to him. Generation after generation a girl is born in this valley with Tatyana's face and auburn hair, and each time he goes out to find her.
Strahd: In each new life I seek her out with patience, letters, gifts and rescues, hoping she will choose me at last. I was gentle with her at the first. When gentleness fails me, I lay courtesy aside and return to the old ways.
Narrator: Each time it ends in terror, in flight or in death, or she gives her heart to someone else and he is left behind, unchanged. In the back pages he keeps a neat ledger of their names and years. He never once asks why she cannot love him.
Narrator: The last entry has no date, only a few lines about a girl in the village below the castle. The steady hand breaks here, and the ink is pressed down so hard that it tears the paper.
Strahd: I chose every step of it, and I would choose it again.
Narrator: The hand never shakes on that last line, which is how you know.
cutscene end
-> END
